    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Remember how you spend a lot of time learning how to
                differentiate in calc 1?
                Well, <m>\nabla</m> is shorthand for differentiation in other
                contexts, so it's important to develop some comfort in computing
                with <m>\nabla</m>.
                Though it may feel like "busy work," that's what this first
                exercise is all about.
                You wil need these identities in subsequent exercises.
            </p>

            <p>
                In class, we saw that <m>\nabla \times \nabla \phi = \vec{0}</m>
                and <m>\nabla \cdot \nabla \times \phi = 0</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    As we saw in class, we usually abbreviate
                    <m>\nabla \cdot \nabla \times \phi</m> as
                    <m>\nabla^2 \phi</m> and call it the <term>Laplacian</term>
                    of <m>\phi</m>.
                    Compute the Laplacian of <m>\phi</m> for
                    <md>
                        \phi(x,y)=x^2-y^2+2xy.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Be patient, and show that for some general field
                    <m>\mbf F</m>:
                    <md>
                        \nabla^2 \mbf F = \nabla(\nabla \cdot \mbf F) - \nabla \times (\nabla \times \mbf F).
                    </md>
                    In this expression, <m>\nabla^2 \mbf F</m> is
                    <m>(\nabla^2 F_1, \nabla^2 F_2, \nabla^2 F_3)</m> for
                    <m>\mbf F = (F_1, F_2, F_3)</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you are comfortable with the notation, you could let
                    <m>\mbf F = (P,Q, R)</m> where <m>P,Q,R</m> are all
                    functions of <m>x,y,z</m>, and denote
                    <m>\frac{\partial P}{\partial x} = P_x, \ldots</m>,
                    otherwise ignore this comment.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\mbf F</m> depends on time <m>t</m> as well as
                    position <m>(x,y,z)</m>, show that
                    <md>
                        \frac{\partial}{\partial t}(\nabla \times \mbf F) = \nabla \times \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\mbf F.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The previous question showed a formula for how the time
                    derivative impacts the curl of a field <m>\mbf F</m>.
                    State similar formulas for the gradient and divergence.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is a function, show the following product rule:
                    <md>
                        \nabla \cdot (f\nabla g) = f\nabla^2 g + \nabla f \cdot \nabla g.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In a vacuum, Maxwell's equations state that
                <md>
                    <mrow>\nabla \cdot \mbf E \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\nabla \times \mbf E \amp = -\frac{\partial \mbf B}{\partial t} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\nabla \cdot \mbf B \amp = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>\nabla \times \mbf B \amp = \frac{1}{c^2}\frac{\partial \mbf E}{\partial t} </mrow>
                </md>
                where <m>\mbf E</m> and <m>\mbf B</m> represent the
                time-dependent electric and magnetic field in a region and
                <m>c</m> is the speed of light.
            </p>

            <p>
                Using the results of problem 1, show that
                <md>
                    \nabla^2 \mbf E = \frac{1}{c^2} \frac{\partial^2 \mbf E}{\partial t^2} \ \ \ \text{     and    } \ \ \ \nabla^2 \mbf B = \frac{1}{c^2} \frac{\partial^2 \mbf B}{\partial t^2}.
                </md>
            </p>

            <p>
                (We'll see later that this says <m>\mbf E</m> and <m>B</m>
                satisfy the <term>wave equation</term>.)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>\mbf F(x,y,z) = (x,y,z)</m> and <m>\sigma</m> is
                the sphere of radius 2 centered at the origin (
                <m>{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4}</m>), and <m>\vec n</m> is the outward
                normal vector.
                Evaluate the flux of <m>\mbf F</m> through <m>\sigma</m> in the
                direction of <m>\vec n</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find the flux of the field <m>\mbf F=(x,y,0)</m> through the
                surface <m>{z=4-x^2-y^2}</m> that lies above the <m>xy-</m> plane
                with outward normal <m>\vec n</m> that points upward.
                This surface is not closed!
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>